</p><p>As a result, the different nuclear spin states have different energies in a non-zero magnetic field. In less formal language, we can talk about the two spin states of a spin <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1214402035"><span class="sfrac">&#8288;<span class="tion"><span class="num">1</span><span class="sr-only">/</span><span class="den">2</span></span>&#8288;</span> as being <i>aligned</i> either with or against the magnetic field. If <i>γ</i> is positive (true for most isotopes used in NMR) then <span class="nowrap"><i>m</i> = <link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1214402035"><span class="sfrac">&#8288;<span class="tion"><span class="num">1</span><span class="sr-only">/</span><span class="den">2</span></span>&#8288;</span></span> ("spin up") is the lower energy state.
</p><p>The energy difference between the two states is:
